WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.762
Welcome to the story deck for
Building the Daily Checks Workbook.

2
00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:08.876
Instead of four chapters one at a time,
we'll follow one fictional workspace,

3
00:00:08.876 --> 00:00:13.741
for contoso dot example, through two
mornings: November sixteenth, 2026,

4
00:00:13.741 --> 00:00:19.101
when something quietly broke overnight,
and the next morning, when it was fixed.

5
00:00:19.101 --> 00:00:22.175
Every value comes from the
kit's own test fixtures,

6
00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:25.700
and every output on a slide is
what the kit really printed.

7
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.531
The workbook has been
reviewed and approved,

8
00:00:28.531 --> 00:00:32.340
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

9
00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.078
Here's the route.

10
00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:41.911
The operator runbook asks for ten daily
checks, most done one portal at a time,

11
00:00:41.911 --> 00:00:44.903
and a quiet portal looks
just like a broken feed.

12
00:00:44.903 --> 00:00:47.753
Scene one turns ten checks into ten tabs.

13
00:00:47.753 --> 00:00:51.103
Scene two catches an export
someone edited in the portal.

14
00:00:51.103 --> 00:00:54.226
Scene three is the morning
of November sixteenth,

15
00:00:54.226 --> 00:00:56.740
when the sign-in logs stopped overnight.

16
00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.515
Scene four is the next morning, and
what the empty queues were hiding.

17
00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:03.565
Scene five tries to file
the picture as evidence.

18
00:01:03.565 --> 00:01:06.960
And scene six deploys
it, the same way twice.

19
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:11.535
Scene one.

20
00:01:11.535 --> 00:01:16.548
The kit's manifest gives each daily
check one row, and the workbook one tab.

21
00:01:16.548 --> 00:01:19.711
Eight tabs read tables in
the Sentinel workspace.

22
00:01:19.711 --> 00:01:20.960
Two are link-outs.

23
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.860
Entra Health's alerts live in the admin
center and in e-mail, not in Log Analytics,

24
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.922
and their G C C availability
is not yet specified,

25
00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:32.597
so the tab sends the operator
there instead of pretending.

26
00:01:32.597 --> 00:01:36.435
And the catalog tasks
live in the I T S M tool.

27
00:01:36.435 --> 00:01:40.860
Two more tabs are partial:
grants, and the log review queue.

28
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.422
Check one.

29
00:01:45.422 --> 00:01:48.173
The risk tab's query returns no rows.

30
00:01:48.173 --> 00:01:49.335
What has it proved?

31
00:01:49.335 --> 00:01:51.310
A: nobody is at risk.

32
00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.918
B: only that its query returned nothing,

33
00:01:53.918 --> 00:01:57.260
which means something only
if its source is fresh.

34
00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.222
Or C: that I D Protection is turned off.

35
00:02:00.222 --> 00:02:03.120
Hold your answer; the
next scenes answer it.

36
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.908
Scene two, before any morning.

37
00:02:08.908 --> 00:02:12.457
Someone edited the workbook in
the portal and exported it.

38
00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:14.415
The validator finds four edits:

39
00:02:14.415 --> 00:02:18.481
a risk query that reads a workspace
path instead of the parameter,

40
00:02:18.481 --> 00:02:21.417
a devices query that now
looks back thirty days,

41
00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:25.633
a grants tab that lost its banner,
and a subscription path in the file.

42
00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:29.482
Microsoft's own advice is to
mask the I D's an export embeds;

43
00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.694
the kit's check simply fails them.

44
00:02:31.694 --> 00:02:32.858
Remember the banner.

45
00:02:32.858 --> 00:02:36.300
It says the workbook is
a view, not evidence.

46
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.425
Who reads the workbook?

47
00:02:41.425 --> 00:02:45.269
Security operations reads
telemetry completeness first,

48
00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:48.563
and opens a high-severity
task for each dark source.

49
00:02:48.563 --> 00:02:54.612
Identity operations reads the sync probe,
risk, emergency access and grants tabs.

50
00:02:54.612 --> 00:02:59.963
Device support works the noncompliant
devices in Intune and the I T S M tool.

51
00:02:59.963 --> 00:03:05.312
The data platform operators triage D L P
alerts in Purview or the Defender portal.

52
00:03:05.312 --> 00:03:08.894
And the security officer reads
nothing on the workbook at all:

53
00:03:08.894 --> 00:03:11.570
they keep the records, never the picture.

54
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.102
The answer to check one is B:

55
00:03:17.102 --> 00:03:20.908
an empty tab proves only that
its query returned nothing.

56
00:03:20.908 --> 00:03:23.321
Scene three: November sixteenth.

57
00:03:23.321 --> 00:03:25.393
At ten past five the evening before,

58
00:03:25.393 --> 00:03:29.682
the Entra diagnostic setting stopped,
and this morning's probe didn't run.

59
00:03:29.682 --> 00:03:34.620
Telemetry flags four rows: three
dark tables and a failing connector.

60
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.470
And look at risk, emergency
access, sync and grants.

61
00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:39.532
They're not clear.

62
00:03:39.532 --> 00:03:40.495
They're stale.

63
00:03:40.495 --> 00:03:43.380
On a plain dashboard,
the risk and emergency

64
00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.110
access tabs would have shown
two calm, empty queues.

65
00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:51.585
Try it.

66
00:03:51.585 --> 00:03:54.377
From the kit's folder,
render the first morning,

67
00:03:54.377 --> 00:03:57.023
then read what the risk
tab's query returned.

68
00:03:57.023 --> 00:03:58.173
No rows at all.

69
00:03:58.173 --> 00:04:03.760
But its source, the sign-in logs, last
arrived at ten past five the evening before.

70
00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.210
Shown as a quiet queue, this is the
morning a risky sign-in goes unseen.

71
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.810
Scene four: the next morning.

72
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.422
The diagnostic setting is
back, and telemetry is clear.

73
00:04:18.422 --> 00:04:20.336
Now the queues show what they were hiding.

74
00:04:20.336 --> 00:04:21.935
One user at high risk.

75
00:04:21.935 --> 00:04:25.201
One emergency access sign-in,
which turns out to be the

76
00:04:25.201 --> 00:04:29.135
quarterly validation drill, and
the review records it as a drill.

77
00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:31.273
And one synchronization conflict.

78
00:04:31.273 --> 00:04:33.262
Grants and D L P alerts are clear,

79
00:04:33.262 --> 00:04:37.980
and this time clear means something,
because their sources are fresh.

80
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.555
Check two.

81
00:04:42.555 --> 00:04:45.730
The first morning's render
shows exactly what went wrong.

82
00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.668
Should it go in the evidence package?

83
00:04:47.668 --> 00:04:50.779
A: yes, it's a dated
picture of the problem.

84
00:04:50.779 --> 00:04:53.943
B: yes, once a second person signs it.

85
00:04:53.943 --> 00:04:58.867
Or C: no; record the checks' own
output, like the reachability check.

86
00:04:58.867 --> 00:05:01.300
The next scene shows what the kit does.

87
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.279
The answer is C.

88
00:05:06.279 --> 00:05:07.200
Scene five.

89
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.963
The kit refuses to record the render:
a rendered view is not evidence.

90
00:05:11.963 --> 00:05:14.571
Instead it records the reachability check,

91
00:05:14.571 --> 00:05:17.976
which asked the workspace for
each table's newest record.

92
00:05:17.976 --> 00:05:22.511
Its result is Fail, and that's the point:
the record keeps what the check found,

93
00:05:22.511 --> 00:05:27.413
tagged with continuous monitoring and
system monitoring and their indicators.

94
00:05:27.413 --> 00:05:32.800
A failing record is evidence of the gap;
a screenshot is only a picture of it.

95
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:37.588
Scene six.

96
00:05:37.588 --> 00:05:41.083
The workbook goes to a test
subscription, then production,

97
00:05:41.083 --> 00:05:44.287
with the same commands and
a different settings file.

98
00:05:44.287 --> 00:05:49.281
The plan previews the change with what-if,
which predicts and changes nothing;

99
00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:52.949
on the fixtures it predicts
one create, a single workbook.

100
00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:55.247
A second person approves the plan's hash,

101
00:05:55.247 --> 00:05:59.075
and the kit deploys nothing else,
and never a plan that would delete.

102
00:05:59.075 --> 00:06:02.387
Production waits until the
test stage's evidence passes.

103
00:06:02.387 --> 00:06:04.782
That's the story: ten checks on one page,

104
00:06:04.782 --> 00:06:09.720
a picture the operator can trust only
because the records behind it are kept.
